Q: How to strip image metadata in the browser before upload (javascript) A: <p>For a pure client-side strip before upload, you generally have three approaches:</p>
<p>### 1) Canvas re-encode (simple; drops most EXIF)<br />
Draw the image to a canvas and export a new Blob. Orientation quirks still need handling if you care about iOS preview.</p>
<pre><code>async function stripViaCanvas(file) {
  const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(file);
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = bitmap.width;
  canvas.height = bitmap.height;
  canvas.getContext('2d').drawImage(bitmap, 0, 0);
  return new Promise((resolve) =&gt;
    canvas.toBlob((blob) =&gt; resolve(new File([blob], file.name, { type: 'image/jpeg' })), 'image/jpeg', 0.92)
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note: this re-encodes pixels (quality tradeoff) and is not a precise “metadata-only” edit.</p>
<h3>2) Binary segment removal / libraries</h3>
<p>Parse JPEG APP1 (EXIF) / XMP segments and rebuild the file (or use something like <code>piexifjs</code> / buffer utilities). Better when you must avoid a full re-encode. C2PA/JUMBF is a separate structure — generic EXIF strippers often miss it.</p>
<h3>3) Ready-made browser tool (no install)</h3>
<p>If you only need an occasional manual clean (not an in-app pipeline), a browser-local metadata remover can strip EXIF/XMP and related fields in-tab: <strong><a href="https://removeailabel.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://removeailabel.com</a></strong><br />
For product upload UIs, prefer (1)/(2) in your own code so you control orientation, quality, and formats.</p>

Q: How to loop through matrix in C# and set all the other elements in the same row and column to zero if you encounter a zero? A: <p>The problem is that setting the other elements of a row or column to 0 yields obviously other zeroes for which you don't want to set their corresponding rows and column to 0.</p>
<p>You solved this problem by doing a first pass where you store the indexes of the zeroes in two consecutive list positions. But the next pass should not loop through the matrix in two nested loops. Instead, you should loop through the list of indexes:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; lst.count; i += 2) { // We step by 2 for the two index values.
    int j = lst[i];     // row index of zero
    int k = lst[i + 1]; // column index of zero

    // Clear row
    for (int col = 0; col &lt; cols; col++) {
        matrix[j][col] = 0;
    }

    // Clear column
    for (int row = 0; row &lt; rows; row++) {
        matrix[row][k] = 0;
    }
}
</code></pre>
